the reason they gave an emotional  resignation speech this week saying that  being Prime Minister had been the honor  of her life and we can speak to someone  who perhaps knows mrs. May more than  most the former Deputy Prime Minister  and university friend with a prime  minister Damian Green thank you very  much for being with us this morning as  someone who's a friend of Teresa may how  was it watching that speech that she  gave when she announced her resignation  no face add that as she said this was a  huge honor to be Prime Minister she  regarded that I think everyone has  commented on her sense of duty which  which was there literally to the end and  and we saw it was a fairly emotional end  to that speech and any decent person  would have thought that's that's very  sad did she speak to you before she made  up her mind no I haven't spoken to in  the last few days but I think to some  extent it became inevitable that the the  deal as devised wasn't gonna go through  Parliament so her prime ministership had  reached the end of the road how do you  think she will be feeling now I mean  obviously she was she was saying how it  was the owner of her life she was  talking about the things that she felt  she'd achieved in office  it felt to me watching it that there was  perhaps a tinge of regret that she  hadn't had enough opportunity to focus  on some of her domestic agenda I think  that's right and and I share that  feeling that we all remember the speech  she made when she went into Downing  Street the attack on the burning  injustice is it felt like a different  type of conservatism one that was  reaching out if you like beyond the  conservative call though beyond the  heartlands for to the whole and now the  attractive vision and because brexit  came to dominate everything she wasn't  able to make anything like enough  progress on any of those things that she  really cared about remembered I guess  too early to say I think to some extent  it depends on on the next leader because  if we can get brexit through and then  move on to a new modern agenda that  looks to the future then it will be seen  as a tough period that maybe we want to  go through but but so in an odd way the  success of the next leader will I think  helped  her place in history well let's talk  about that who do you think should be  the next leader I think the next leader  should be Matt Hancock I do think we  need a fresh start  I think we need somebody obviously  getting brexit through is the immediate  task but we've got to start thinking and  caring about more than that looking  through to the 2020s and I think it's  important for the Conservative Party now  which is done is doing so badly among  young voters in particular to have  somebody who can can speak the language  of those who grew up in the digital  world and who evidently is competent and  on top of issues of technology and how  it applies to people's real lives as  health secretary he's shown that having  new advanced technology in the NHS  actually improves healthcare so you can  apply this knowledge to a wide range of  of the key public services that we as  conservators have got to show we've got  answers to listen to what you're saying  and also to his kind of pitch yesterday  it's clear that he's trying to position  himself as a leader for the future but  realistically is he ever gonna win with  the membership I think we can have a  quick look and at a poll of members  which this is back in January but if  anything I'd imagine that the No Deal  support has grown 57% saying they're  brexit preference is No Deal I mean are  they really going to back someone who  supported remain in the referendum well  I think one of the if you like that the  most dangerous pieces of analysis for  the future the Conservative Party is  simply to keep people in a spec saying  what you did in June 2016 is all that  matters so I can see that that's what  you would like the situation to be but  in reality that we are fighting on  brexit now aren't we well we've got to  get brexit through that that's  absolutely clear but one of the that  mean the problem with No Deal as we all  know is that you won't get that through  the House of Commons so if you have  people who are promising and No Deal  brexit then the the extreme likelihood  is that that various people will find  ways of stopping it going to the House  of Commons and therefore you will have  to go to a general election and I think  those are nice the country certainly  doesn't want that  like the conservative part well I think  he  put it this way there has to be the  possibility that we would see Jeremy  Corbyn in Downing Street and clearly no  conservative wants that so we have to  approach the the brexit conundrum in an  intelligent way you've written today in  a piece in The Sunday Times and the  future survival of the Conservative  Party is at risk a polarizing leader  would be the final straw if a future  survival is at risk is that what I feel  that that very very strongly you've seen  the figures now that the average age at  which people are more like he's vote  conservative than not has gone up to 51  you know we talk about young voters  we're talking about anyone under the age  of 50 here so it's absolutely kids but  it's one of the reasons I'm supporting  that Hancock that I think the  combination of the policy ideas he has  and his approach and style to politics  can actually expand the the conservative  vote in a way that is of supreme  importance to the future the party at  the end half of that quote a polarizing  leader will be the final straw who could  she be talking about well I'll be  talking about any of the candidates who  go down that route we have at the start  so anyone who is talking about leaving  with no deal at the end of October is  that what you defined as polarizing  somebody who says that you know some  conservatives are less good than other  conservatives when one of the other  things I say in in masculine Sunday  times is that the Conservative Party is  at its best and the Conservative Party  only joined many years ago was a party  where Dan Hannon and Dominic grieve can  feel equally at home because actually we  agree on lots of things we don't agree  between ourselves on brexit that's why  we've got to we've got to get out of the  European Union we've got to respect the  referendum it would be much better to do  that smoothly with the deal and then get  on to the many other things that people  really care about in their daily lives  okay let's let's stop skirting around  that you talk about Boris Johnson no I'm  not talking about poet I think it's very  very important that particularly at the  phase where we've got many candidates in  this election that we set out the  principles so you give very much I think  there are many of the people who are  running would be good prime ministers  there's actually there's there's a wide  field  but also a good field and I think the  best of that field is Matt Hancock and  one of the rules I'm going to operate  through this campaign is that I am NOT  going to diss colleagues Ronald Reagan  had this with this famous rule that you  should never be rude about another  Republican in public and I'm going to  apply that to conservatives as well

